$post_body = <<<'HTML'
<p>Honeycomb shades lower energy bills more than flat shades because their accordion-style cells trap a layer of still air against the glass, cutting heat transfer in both summer and winter. Flat shades and flat blinds have no insulating pocket, so a window stays a weak point in a home's envelope no matter how tightly the slats close. The <a href="%BASE%/window-treatments/shades/honeycomb-shades/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">honeycomb shades</a> stocked by Creative Blinds and Drapes are built around this cellular structure, which is why homeowners replacing flat blinds often notice a change in room comfort within the first season.</p>
%IMAGE1%
<h2 id="how-do-the-honeycomb-cells-actually-block-heat">How Do the Honeycomb Cells Actually Block Heat?</h2>
<p>Flat shades, whether roller or Roman style, hang as a single layer of fabric. Honeycomb shades instead form rows of sealed air pockets between the glass and the room, and trapped air is one of the best insulators available in a window treatment. That difference shows up directly on a utility bill, and <a href="%BASE%/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Creative Blinds and Drapes</a> sees it play out the same way on nearly every job:</p>
<ul class="bp-list">
  <li>A single-cell honeycomb shade typically adds a meaningful R-value to a window, compared to almost none from a flat panel.</li>
  <li>Double-cell versions trap two layers of air instead of one, so the resistance to heat flow increases again.</li>
  <li>The cells hold warm air near the glass in winter and block radiant heat in summer, so a furnace or air conditioner cycles less often.</li>
  <li>The biggest gains usually show up on west and south-facing windows, where summer heat gain is highest.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="why-flat-shades-fall-short-once-the-season-changes">Why Flat Shades Fall Short Once the Season Changes</h2>
<p>Flat shades do add some shade and privacy, but they do not change the thermal behavior of the glass itself. A flat roller shade pulled down on a January morning still leaves a wide gap for the cold air near the glass to settle into the room.</p>
<ul class="bp-list">
  <li>Flat fabric creates almost no dead air space, so it does not slow heat loss the way a cellular structure does.</li>
  <li>Gaps along the sides of a flat shade let conditioned air leak past the edges more easily than a shade built for a tighter track fit.</li>
  <li>A single flat panel has no second air layer, so a double-cell honeycomb shade will always outperform it on paper R-value.</li>
  <li>The <a href="%BASE%/guidelines/climate-light-control/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Climate and Light Control guide</a> walks through how shade type interacts with sun exposure on each side of a house.</li>
</ul>
%IMAGE2%
<h2 id="what-does-this-mean-for-a-typical-utility-bill">What Does This Mean for a Typical Utility Bill?</h2>
<p>Heating and cooling make up a large share of a home's energy use, and windows are one of the biggest sources of loss in an older house. Swapping flat shades for honeycomb shades on the windows that get the most sun or the coldest draft typically delivers:</p>
<ul class="bp-list">
  <li>A noticeable drop in the draft felt near the glass on cold mornings.</li>
  <li>Less afternoon heat buildup in rooms with west-facing glass during summer.</li>
  <li>Fewer thermostat adjustments throughout the day, since indoor temperature holds steadier.</li>
  <li>A payback period that is often faster than other home efficiency upgrades, since installation is quick and does not require construction work.</li>
</ul>
<p>Flat shades still have a place in a home, but for windows where energy loss is the real concern, honeycomb shades are the more effective choice, since the air they trap matters more than the fabric itself. A short visit from the team is the easiest way to find out which rooms would benefit most, and anyone ready to compare options can <a href="%BASE%/contact/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">schedule a free in-home consultation</a> to get specific numbers for their own windows. For a broader look at how the two categories stack up outside of energy use, our guide on <a href="%BASE%/blog/are-blinds-or-shades-better/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">whether blinds or shades perform better</a> covers light control, privacy, and style side by side.</p>
HTML;

